<!DOCTYPE html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="UTF-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1.0"><title>Special Relativity — Lorentz Transformations, Spacetime Interval &amp; Relativistic Dynamics | vlymbooq</title><meta name="description" content="Deep dive into Special Relativity — Einstein's two postulates, the Lorentz transformations, time dilation, length contraction, the relativity of simultaneity, the Minkowski spacetime diagram, the spacetime interval and its classification (timelike, lightlike, spacelike), proper time, four-vectors, the four-velocity and four-momentum, relativistic energy and momentum, E = mc², and relativistic kinematics and collisions."><style>@import url('https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Inter:wght@400;500;600;700;800;900&display=swap');*{margin:0;padding:0;box-sizing:border-box}:root{--bg:#09090b;--bg-card:#111113;--border:rgba(255,255,255,.06);--text:#fafafa;--text-sec:#a1a1aa;--text-muted:#52525b;--purple:#a78bfa;--emerald:#34d399;--red:#ef4444;--radius:12px}body{font-family:Inter,sans-serif;background:var(--bg);color:var(--text)}a{color:var(--purple);text-decoration:none}.nav{position:sticky;top:0;z-index:100;padding:14px 24px;background:rgba(9,9,11,.85);backdrop-filter:blur(16px);border-bottom:1px solid var(--border)}.nav-inner{max-width:900px;margin:0 auto;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between}.brand-text{font-weight:800;background:linear-gradient(135deg,#6366f1,var(--emerald));-webkit-background-clip:text;-webkit-text-fill-color:transparent}.nav-links a{padding:7px 14px;border-radius:100px;font-size:.82em;color:#a1a1aa}.container{max-width:900px;margin:0 auto;padding:24px}.lesson-body h2{font-size:1.5em;font-weight:900;margin:32px 0 12px}.lesson-body p{color:var(--text-sec);font-size:.92em;line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:12px}.btn{padding:8px 20px;border-radius:100px;font-weight:600;font-size:.82em;border:none;cursor:pointer}.btn-primary{background:rgba(167,139,250,.15);color:var(--purple)}.btn-success{background:rgba(52,211,153,.15);color:var(--emerald)}.story-card{background:linear-gradient(135deg,rgba(96,165,250,.06),rgba(167,139,250,.04));border:1px solid rgba(167,139,250,.15);border-radius:var(--radius);padding:20px;margin-bottom:20px;position:relative;overflow:hidden}.story-label{font-size:.72em;font-weight:600;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1.2px;color:var(--purple);margin-bottom:3px}.story-title{font-size:1.05em;font-weight:700;margin-bottom:8px;line-height:1.4;color:var(--text)}.story-text{font-size:.85em;line-height:1.7;color:var(--text-sec);margin-bottom:12px}.story-moral{font-size:.82em;font-style:italic;color:var(--emerald);padding:10px 14px;background:rgba(52,211,153,.06);border-radius:8px;border-left:3px solid var(--emerald)}.section-label{display:inline-block;font-size:.72em;font-weight:700;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1.5px;padding:4px 14px;border-radius:100px;margin:28px 0 4px}.part1{color:#60a5fa;background:rgba(96,165,250,.1)}.part2{color:var(--purple);background:rgba(167,139,250,.1)}.part3{color:var(--emerald);background:rgba(52,211,153,.1)}.part4{color:#f97316;background:rgba(249,115,22,.1)}.section-divider{height:1px;background:var(--border);margin:32px 0}.quote-box{background:linear-gradient(135deg,rgba(167,139,250,.08),rgba(52,211,153,.04));border:1px solid rgba(167,139,250,.15);border-radius:var(--radius);padding:20px;margin-bottom:20px}.quote{font-size:1em;font-weight:500;font-style:italic;color:var(--text);margin-bottom:6px}.attribution{font-size:.82em;color:var(--text-muted)}.q-card{background:var(--bg-card);border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:var(--radius);padding:20px;margin-bottom:16px}.q-text{font-weight:600;font-size:.92em;margin-bottom:12px;line-height:1.5}.q-opts{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:8px}.q-opt{padding:10px 14px;border-radius:8px;border:1px solid var(--border);cursor:pointer;font-size:.85em;color:var(--text-sec);transition:all .2s}.q-opt:hover{background:rgba(167,139,250,.08);border-color:rgba(167,139,250,.3)}.q-opt.correct{background:rgba(52,211,153,.15);border-color:var(--emerald);color:var(--emerald)}.q-opt.wrong{background:rgba(239,68,68,.15);border-color:var(--red);color:var(--red)}.q-opt.disabled{pointer-events:none;opacity:.6}.q-soln{display:none;margin-top:12px;padding:12px;background:rgba(167,139,250,.06);border-radius:8px;font-size:.82em;color:var(--text-sec);line-height:1.6}.q-opt.correct~.q-soln,.q-opt.wrong~.q-soln{display:block}.q-soln.show{display:block}</style></head><body><nav class="nav"><div class="nav-inner"><a href="index.html"><span class="brand-text">vlymbooq</span></a><div class="nav-links"><a href="index.html">Home</a><a href="math.html">Math Map</a><a href="physics.html">Physics Map</a></div></div></nav><div class="container"><div class="lesson-body"><h2>Special Relativity — Lorentz Transformations, Spacetime Interval &amp; Relativistic Dynamics</h2><p>Special relativity, formulated by Albert Einstein in 1905, revolutionized our understanding of space, time, and motion. It rests on two <strong>postulates</strong>: (1) the laws of physics are the same in all inertial reference frames (the principle of relativity), and (2) the speed of light in vacuum is the same for all inertial observers regardless of the motion of the source. The <strong>Lorentz transformations</strong> replace the Galilean transformations: x' = γ(x − vt), t' = γ(t − vx/c²), where γ = 1/√(1 − v²/c²) is the Lorentz factor. These transformations lead to three startling consequences: <strong>time dilation</strong> — moving clocks run slow (Δt' = γ Δτ); <strong>length contraction</strong> — moving objects contract along the direction of motion (L = L₀/γ); and the <strong>relativity of simultaneity</strong> — events simultaneous in one frame are not simultaneous in another. The <strong>Minkowski spacetime</strong> formalism treats space and time as a unified four-dimensional manifold. The <strong>spacetime interval</strong> (Δs)² = (cΔt)² − (Δx)² − (Δy)² − (Δz)² is invariant under Lorentz transformations. Intervals are classified as <strong>timelike</strong> (Δs² > 0, causally connected), <strong>lightlike</strong> (Δs² = 0, light-speed separation), and <strong>spacelike</strong> (Δs² < 0, no causal connection). The <strong>proper time</strong> τ is the time measured in the rest frame: dτ² = dt² − (dx²+dy²+dz²)/c². <strong>Four-vectors</strong> (such as position X^{μ} = (ct, x, y, z), four-velocity U^{μ} = dx^{μ}/dτ = γ(c, v), and four-momentum P^{μ} = m₀U^{μ} = (E/c, p)) transform via the Lorentz group. The relativistic energy-momentum relation: E² = (pc)² + (m₀c²)², and the famous equation E = mc² for a particle at rest.</p><div class="quote-box"><div class="quote">"Time and space are modes by which we think, not conditions in which we live."</div><div class="attribution">— Albert Einstein</div></div><div class="section-label part1">Part 1</div><h2>🚀 Einstein's Postulates and the Lorentz Transformations</h2><div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">Foundations</div><div class="story-title">The Principle of Relativity, the Constancy of Light, Lorentz Transformations, and the Relativity of Simultaneity</div><div class="story-text">Einstein's <strong>first postulate</strong> (the principle of relativity): the laws of physics take the same form in all inertial frames of reference. No experiment can detect absolute motion. The <strong>second postulate</strong>: the speed of light in vacuum c = 299,792,458 m/s is constant for all inertial observers, independent of the motion of the source. These postulates are incompatible with Galilean relativity (which predicts c ± v). The solution: the <strong>Lorentz transformations</strong> (previously discovered by Lorentz in 1904 to explain the Michelson-Morley null result). For a frame S' moving at velocity v along the x-axis relative to S: x' = γ(x − vt), y' = y, z' = z, t' = γ(t − vx/c²), where γ = 1/√(1 − v²/c²). The inverse transformations: x = γ(x' + vt'), t = γ(t' + vx'/c²). When v ≪ c, γ → 1 and the transformations reduce to Galilean: x' = x − vt, t' = t. The <strong>relativity of simultaneity</strong>: two events that are simultaneous in S (t₁ = t₂ for x₁ ≠ x₂) are not simultaneous in S': t₁' = γ(t₁ − vx₁/c²) ≠ γ(t₂ − vx₂/c²) = t₂'. This is not a measurement artifact — simultaneity is genuinely frame-dependent. <strong>Time dilation</strong>: a clock at rest in S' ticks at proper time Δτ; in S, it appears to tick more slowly: Δt = γΔτ. The <strong>twin paradox</strong>: one twin travels at relativistic speed and returns younger — resolved by noting the traveling twin undergoes acceleration and is not in an inertial frame throughout. <strong>Length contraction</strong> (Lorentz-FitzGerald contraction): the length of an object moving relative to an observer is contracted along the direction of motion: L = L₀/γ, where L₀ is the proper length. This is a real effect — for example, muons created in the upper atmosphere (τ ≈ 2.2 μs) reach the Earth's surface despite traveling at close to c because their decay time is dilated.</div><div class="story-moral">The Lorentz transformations encode the relativity of simultaneity, time dilation, and length contraction — all consequences of the invariant speed of light — and reduce to Galilean transformations in the low-velocity limit.</div></div><div class="section-label part2">Part 2</div><h2>📐 Minkowski Spacetime and the Spacetime Interval</h2><div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">Geometry</div><div class="story-title">Spacetime Diagrams, the Spacetime Interval, Light Cones, Proper Time, and Causality</div><div class="story-text"><strong>Minkowski spacetime</strong> (1908) unifies space and time into a four-dimensional manifold with metric η_{μν} = diag(1, −1, −1, −1) (the Minkowski metric). An event is a point (t, x, y, z) in spacetime. The <strong>spacetime interval</strong> between two events: (Δs)² = (cΔt)² − (Δx)² − (Δy)² − (Δz)² is invariant under Lorentz transformations — the same for all inertial observers. This is analogous to the invariant distance in Euclidean geometry but with a minus sign. Classification of intervals: <strong>Timelike</strong> (Δs² > 0): (cΔt)² > (Δx)², a causal connection is possible; there exists a frame where both events occur at the same place. <strong>Lightlike</strong> (Δs² = 0): events are connected by a light signal; Δx = cΔt. <strong>Spacelike</strong> (Δs² < 0): no causal connection possible; there exists a frame where both events are simultaneous. The <strong>light cone</strong> at an event: all points connected by light rays — the boundary between the future (timelike, Δt > 0), the past (timelike, Δt < 0), and elsewhere (spacelike). Events inside the future light cone are causally accessible; events outside cannot influence the event. The <strong>proper time</strong> τ along a timelike worldline: dτ² = dt² − (dx²+dy²+dz²)/c² = dt²/γ². The proper time is the time measured by a clock moving along the worldline. For a timelike path, the proper time is maximized for inertial (straight) worldlines — the <strong>twin paradox</strong> as a consequence of the geometry of spacetime. The <strong>worldline</strong> of a particle is its path through spacetime. The <strong>four-velocity</strong> U^{μ} = dx^{μ}/dτ = γ(c, v), satisfying η_{μν}U^{μ}U^{ν} = c². The <strong>four-acceleration</strong> A^{μ} = dU^{μ}/dτ. The <strong>relativistic Doppler effect</strong>: for light emitted by a source moving at speed v at angle θ to the line of sight: f_obs = f_src / [γ(1 − (v/c) cos θ)]. For a source moving directly away (θ = 180°): f_obs = f_src √((1 − β)/(1 + β)) — the relativistic redshift.</div><div class="story-moral">Minkowski spacetime provides the geometric framework for special relativity — the invariant spacetime interval replaces the Euclidean distance, and the light cone structure encodes causality in a frame-independent way.</div></div><div class="section-label part3">Part 3</div><h2>💥 Relativistic Dynamics: Energy, Momentum, and Mass-Energy Equivalence</h2><div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">Dynamics</div><div class="story-title">Relativistic Momentum, Relativistic Energy, E = mc², Conservation Laws, and Relativistic Collisions</div><div class="story-text">In special relativity, the Newtonian definition of momentum p = mv must be modified to ensure conservation in all inertial frames. The <strong>relativistic momentum</strong>: p = γmv = mv/√(1 − v²/c²). The <strong>relativistic energy</strong>: E = γmc² = mc²/√(1 − v²/c²). At rest (v = 0), E₀ = mc² — the famous <strong>mass-energy equivalence</strong>. Kinetic energy: K = E − mc² = (γ − 1)mc². For v ≪ c: K ≈ ½mv² + (3/8)mv⁴/c² + ... — the Newtonian kinetic energy plus relativistic corrections. The <strong>energy-momentum relation</strong>: E² = (pc)² + (mc²)². In natural units (c = 1): E² = p² + m². For massless particles (photons): E = pc, p = E/c = h/λ. Conservation of four-momentum: in any interaction, the total four-momentum P^{μ}_{total} = (E_total/c, p_total) is conserved. This unifies energy and momentum conservation. For a <strong>relativistic collision</strong>, the invariant mass of a system: M² = (Σ E_i/c²)² − (Σ p_i/c)² is conserved and frame-independent. Particle decay: if a particle of mass M at rest decays into two particles of masses m₁ and m₂, the energies are E₁ = (M² + m₁² − m₂²)c²/(2M) and similarly for E₂. The <strong>Compton effect</strong>: a photon scattering off an electron: λ' − λ = (h/m₀c)(1 − cos θ), where h/m₀c is the Compton wavelength of the electron (2.43 × 10⁻¹² m). This demonstrated the particle nature of photons. The <strong>center-of-momentum frame</strong> (zero total momentum) is the most convenient for analyzing collisions. The <strong>Mandelstam variables</strong> s, t, u provide Lorentz-invariant descriptions of scattering processes. The <strong>photon rocket</strong>: the ultimate efficiency limit for propulsion — the relativistic rocket equation accounts for the decreasing mass of the rocket as fuel is converted to energy.</div><div class="story-moral">Relativistic dynamics extends conservation of energy and momentum to all reference frames, with E = mc² revealing the equivalence of mass and energy — the basis for nuclear reactions and particle physics.</div></div><div class="section-label part4">Part 4</div><h2>🧪 Advanced Topics: Four-Vectors, Lorentz Group, and Experimental Tests</h2><div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">Advanced</div><div class="story-title">Four-Vectors, Tensors, the Lorentz Group, the Relativistic Lagrangian, and Experimental Tests of Special Relativity</div><div class="story-text">The <strong>Lorentz group</strong> is the group of transformations preserving the Minkowski metric. It consists of boosts (transformations between moving frames) and rotations. The <strong>proper orthochronous Lorentz group</strong> SO⁺(1,3) (det = 1, preserves time direction) is the physically relevant component. The <strong>Poincaré group</strong> adds spacetime translations — it is the full symmetry group of special relativity (10 generators: 3 boosts + 3 rotations + 4 translations). <strong>Four-vectors</strong> are objects that transform under the Lorentz group as X^{μ} → Λ^{μ}_{ν} X^{ν}. The scalar product is Lorentz-invariant: A·B = η_{μν}A^{μ}B^{ν} = A⁰B⁰ − A¹B¹ − A²B² − A³B³. Important four-vectors: position X^{μ} = (ct, r), four-velocity U^{μ} = γ(c, v), four-momentum P^{μ} = (E/c, p), four-force (Minkowski force) F^{μ} = dP^{μ}/dτ = γ(f·v/c, f), where f is the Newtonian force. The <strong>electromagnetic four-potential</strong>: A^{μ} = (φ/c, A). The <strong>relativistic Lagrangian</strong> for a free particle: L = −mc²/γ = −mc²√(1 − v²/c²). The action S = ∫ L dt is Lorentz-invariant. The Lagrangian for a charged particle in an EM field: L = −mc²√(1 − v²/c²) + q(A·v − φ). The relativistic Hamilton-Jacobi equation leads to the Klein-Gordon equation for spin-0 particles. The relativistic conservation laws for energy and momentum are encapsulated in the conservation of the energy-momentum tensor: ∂_μ T^{μν} = 0. Experimental tests of special relativity: the <strong>Michelson-Morley experiment</strong> (1887) — null result for the ether wind (the most famous failed experiment). The <strong>Kennedy-Thorndike experiment</strong> (1932) — confirmed that the speed of light is independent of the velocity of the apparatus. The <strong>Hafele-Keating experiment</strong> (1971) — atomic clocks flown on airplanes showed time dilation effects matching special and general relativity predictions. The <strong>Ives-Stilwell experiment</strong> (1938) — confirmed the relativistic Doppler effect. Particle accelerators routinely confirm relativistic dynamics: the muon lifetime in storage rings matches the time dilation prediction; the increase of electron mass with velocity in cyclotrons necessitates synchrotrons. The <strong>Global Positioning System (GPS)</strong> must correct for both special relativistic (time dilation from satellite motion) and general relativistic (gravitational time dilation) effects — a practical everyday application of relativity.</div><div class="story-moral">The Lorentz group and four-vector formalism provide the mathematical structure of special relativity; every experiment to date has confirmed its predictions, and GPS is a daily testament to the necessity of relativistic corrections.</div></div><div class="section-divider"></div><h2 style="margin-top:0">🎯 Practice</h2><div id="practice-qs"></div><div style="margin-top:16px"><button class="btn btn-primary" onclick="resetPractice()">🔄 Reset All</button><a href="physics.html" class="btn btn-success">← Back to Physics Map</a></div></div></div><script>var practiceQs=[{id:45073,text:"🚀 The two postulates of special relativity are:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"The laws of physics are the same in all inertial frames, and the speed of light is constant for all inertial observers",c:true},{l:"B",t:"The speed of light is constant, and time is absolute",c:false},{l:"C",t:"All motion is relative, and the speed of light depends on the source motion",c:false},{l:"D",t:"Energy is conserved, and the speed of light is the maximum speed",c:false}],sol:"Einstein's <strong>two postulates</strong>: (1) the principle of relativity — the laws of physics are the same in all inertial frames; (2) the constancy of the speed of light — c is the same in all inertial frames, independent of source motion."},{id:45074,text:"🚀 The Lorentz factor γ for a velocity v is:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"γ = 1/√(1 − v²/c²)",c:true},{l:"B",t:"γ = √(1 − v²/c²)",c:false},{l:"C",t:"γ = 1/(1 − v²/c²)",c:false},{l:"D",t:"γ = 1 − v²/c²",c:false}],sol:"The <strong>Lorentz factor</strong>: γ = 1/√(1 − v²/c²). As v → c, γ → ∞. For v = 0.6c, γ = 1.25; for v = 0.99c, γ ≈ 7.09."},{id:45075,text:"🚀 The Lorentz transformation for the time coordinate between frames moving with velocity v along the x-axis is:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"t' = γ(t − vx/c²)",c:true},{l:"B",t:"t' = γ(t − vx)",c:false},{l:"C",t:"t' = γ(t + vx/c²)",c:false},{l:"D",t:"t' = t/γ",c:false}],sol:"The <strong>Lorentz transformation for time</strong>: t' = γ(t − vx/c²). The term vx/c² is responsible for the relativity of simultaneity."},{id:45076,text:"🚀 Time dilation means that a moving clock appears to run slow by a factor of:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Δt = γ Δτ (moving clock ticks less frequently)",c:true},{l:"B",t:"Δt = Δτ/γ",c:false},{l:"C",t:"Δt = Δτ",c:false},{l:"D",t:"Δt = γ² Δτ",c:false}],sol:"<strong>Time dilation</strong>: Δt = γ Δτ, where Δτ is the proper time (measured in the clock's rest frame). So 1 second on a moving clock corresponds to γ seconds in the lab frame."},{id:45077,text:"📐 The spacetime interval (Δs)² = (cΔt)² − (Δx)² − (Δy)² − (Δz)² is:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Invariant under Lorentz transformations",c:true},{l:"B",t:"Invariant under Galilean transformations",c:false},{l:"C",t:"Always positive",c:false},{l:"D",t":"Frame-dependent",c:false}],sol:"The <strong>spacetime interval</strong> is Lorentz-invariant — all inertial observers agree on its value. This is the fundamental invariant quantity in special relativity, analogous to the Euclidean distance."},{id:45078,text:"📐 A timelike interval (Δs² > 0) means:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"A causal connection between the events is possible",c:true},{l:"B",t:"No causal connection is possible",c:false},{l:"C",t:"The events are connected by a light signal",c:false},{l:"D",t:"The events are simultaneous in some frame",c:false}],sol:"A <strong>timelike interval</strong> (Δs² > 0) means (cΔt)² > (Δr)², so a signal traveling at ≤ c can connect the events — a causal connection is possible."},{id:45079,text:"📐 The proper time τ along a timelike worldline is defined by:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"dτ² = dt² − dr²/c²",c:true},{l:"B",t:"dτ² = dt² + dr²/c²",c:false},{l:"C",t:"dτ = dt/γ²",c:false},{l:"D",t:"dτ = γ dt",c:false}],sol:"The <strong>proper time</strong>: dτ² = dt² − dr²/c² = dt²/γ². For a clock moving along its worldline, dτ = dt/γ, so proper time is less than coordinate time for moving clocks."},{id:45080,text:"💥 The relativistic momentum of a particle of mass m moving with velocity v is:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"p = γmv",c:true},{l:"B",t:"p = mv",c:false},{l:"C",t:"p = γmv²",c:false},{l:"D",t:"p = γ²mv",c:false}],sol:"<strong>Relativistic momentum</strong>: p = γmv = mv/√(1 − v²/c²). This reduces to p = mv when v ≪ c and ensures momentum conservation in all inertial frames."},{id:45081,text:"💥 The relativistic energy of a particle with rest mass m and momentum p satisfies:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"E² = (pc)² + (mc²)²",c:true},{l:"B",t:"E = pc + mc²",c:false},{l:"C",t:"E² = p²c² − m²c⁴",c:false},{l:"D",t:"E = mc² + ½mv²",c:false}],sol:"The <strong>energy-momentum relation</strong>: E² = (pc)² + (mc²)². For a particle at rest (p = 0), E = mc². For a photon (m = 0), E = pc."},{id:45082,text:"💥 The kinetic energy of a relativistic particle is:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"K = (γ − 1)mc²",c:true},{l:"B",t:"K = ½γmv²",c:false},{l:"C",t:"K = (γ² − 1)mc²",c:false},{l:"D",t:"K = γmc²",c:false}],sol:"<strong>Relativistic kinetic energy</strong>: K = E − mc² = (γ − 1)mc². For v ≪ c, expanding γ ≈ 1 + v²/(2c²) gives K ≈ ½mv²."},{id:45083,text:"🧪 The Lorentz group is the group of all transformations that preserve:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"The Minkowski metric η_{μν} = diag(1, −1, −1, −1)",c:true},{l:"B",t:"The Euclidean metric δ_{ij}",c:false},{l:"C",t:"The speed of light and Newton's laws",c:false},{l:"D",t:"The Galilean transformations",c:false}],sol:"The <strong>Lorentz group</strong> consists of all linear transformations Λ that satisfy Λ^T η Λ = η, preserving the Minkowski metric and thus the spacetime interval."},{id:45084,text:"🧪 The Hafele-Keating experiment (1971) tested special relativity by:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Comparing atomic clocks flown on airplanes with stationary clocks",c:true},{l:"B",t:"Measuring the speed of light in moving water",c:false},{l:"C",t:"Observing muon decay in the atmosphere",c:false},{l:"D",t:"Using a Michelson interferometer",c:false}],sol:"The <strong>Hafele-Keating experiment</strong> flew cesium atomic clocks on commercial airliners. The clocks showed time differences consistent with special relativity (velocity time dilation) and general relativity (gravitational time dilation)."}];var answered={},correctCount=0;function renderPractice(){var c=document.getElementById('practice-qs'),h='';for(var i=0;i<practiceQs.length;i++){var q=practiceQs[i],o='';for(var j=0;j<q.opts.length;j++){o+='<div class="q-opt" onclick="selectOpt('+q.id+','+j+')">'+q.opts[j].l+'. '+q.opts[j].t+'</div>';}h+='<div class="q-card" id="q-'+q.id+'"><div class="q-text">'+(i+1)+'. '+q.text+'</div><div class="q-opts">'+o+'</div><div class="q-soln" id="soln-'+q.id+'"><strong>'+getCorrectLabel(q)+'</strong><br>'+q.sol+'</div></div>';}c.innerHTML=h;}function getCorrectLabel(q){for(var i=0;i<q.opts.length;i++){if(q.opts[i].c)return q.opts[i].l+'. '+q.opts[i].t;}return '';}function selectOpt(qId,idx){if(answered[qId])return;answered[qId]=true;var q=practiceQs.filter(function(x){return x.id===qId})[0];var opts=document.querySelectorAll('#q-'+qId+' .q-opt'),isCorrect=q.opts[idx].c;for(var i=0;i<opts.length;i++)opts[i].classList.add('disabled');if(isCorrect){opts[idx].classList.add('correct');correctCount++;}else{opts[idx].classList.add('wrong');for(var i=0;i<q.opts.length;i++){if(q.opts[i].c)opts[i].classList.add('correct');}}document.getElementById('soln-'+qId).classList.add('show');}function resetPractice(){if(!confirm('Reset?'))return;answered={};correctCount=0;document.querySelectorAll('.q-opt').forEach(function(e){e.className='q-opt'});document.querySelectorAll('.q-soln').forEach(function(e){e.classList.remove('show')});renderPractice();}renderPractice();</script></body></html>